+## Rejected (with reason)
+- §3.5 hash check; §2.4 "No new precondition machinery" — apply never recomputes the hash from the stored plan file, so a rewrite of `.graph-local/plans/<pl>.json` passes. Rejected 3/3: code premise true (src/plan/apply.rs step 2 compares `stored.hash`), but an actor with filesystem access to the instance can already `git commit` directly or call the socket, both accepted §3.5 residuals; plan-store tamper-resistance is pre-existing and out of this spec's scope. Possible follow-up: recompute the hash at apply.
+- §3.3 relay flow; §3.5 hash-compare sentence — the harness prompt shows only the command line, so an agent can present a forged benign diff next to the real hash. Rejected 2/3: §3.5 declares the gate cooperative and accepts strictly stronger bypasses (socket calls, rewraps, settings edits) that skip the prompt entirely; a forged display adds no capability. Reproduce seat's Claude-docs evidence (read-only `echo`/`cat` run without a prompt; prompt shows only command text) is accurate but answered by the scope argument. Optional hardening: a user-run `plan show <pl>`, or list forged display in §3.5.
+- §3.2 Codex rules syntax; §3.4 "the owned rules file exists" — unverified Codex `prefix_rule` syntax and loading; doctor checks existence only. Rejected 3/3: reproduce seat ran `codex execpolicy check --rules h.rules herdr-graph apply pl_x --confirm h` on codex-cli 0.163.0 and got `decision: prompt`; docs (https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/rules) confirm `rules/` is scanned under every active config layer at startup. Prompt-skipping approval policies are an accepted §3.5 residual; acceptance criterion 4 asks only that doctor warn when the rule is absent.
+- §1.3/§1.4 `request complete --summary-from` unauthenticated, first write wins — Rejected 2/3: summaries are `data` (§4.2, §4.5), not in the instruction stack; the path is daemon-derived to one fixed file; the old unchecked `request complete --output` already let any caller complete any request. Ground seat confirmed at Nit (no authorization rule stated). Optional: list under §3.5 residuals or restrict completion to the assignee.
+- §3.5 residual list — cross-pane keystroke injection (`herdr pane send-keys`) can answer another seat's approval prompt. Rejected 2/3: same trust class as accepted residuals (socket calls, settings edits); ground seat confirmed at Nit as a one-line omission from the documented list. Documentation suggestion for SKILL.md/README.
+- §3.4 "Doctor reads the same config paths setup uses"; "Harnesses without the owned hook are skipped" — seats under other CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/CODEX_HOME profiles go ungated and unreported. Rejected 3/3: §3.5 states the dependency ("depends on every seat's harness having the rule") and calls the gate cooperative; acceptance criterion 4 scopes doctor to installed harnesses; per-seat profile auditing is a stricter system than specified. Documentation nit: add "profile where setup never ran" to §3.5.
+- §2.2 `--from <path>`; §2.4 recompute — capture vs re-read of `--from` at apply undefined. Rejected 2/3: `after_sha` is in the compared effect, so approved bytes equal applied bytes either way; a deleted file fails closed. Ground seat confirmed at Should-fix (transport unpinned, §1.4 pins it for summaries). Minor wording nit for the leaf: say the CLI reads the file and the plan carries the bytes.
+- §2.2 `--from <path>` daemon-side read (confused deputy) — Rejected 2/3: confidentiality of host files is not a stated goal; §3.5 accepts direct socket calls, which already let any agent have the daemon act for it; the existing template kind already reads `--from` in the daemon (src/templates/kinds.rs `read_json_arg`). Ground seat dissented at Should-fix citing the same code and §1.4's CLI-reads-bytes precedent; the majority acknowledged that premise and rejected on scope, so the default stands. Recommended follow-up: pin the CLI as the reader and carry bytes in the plan (also resolves the capture-vs-reread nit above).
+- §1.3 `summary_conflict` with no repair path — Rejected 2/3: §1.1 keeps `--output` working unchanged, so a stranded request is completed by pointing `--output` at the existing file; overwrite-on-retry was weighed and rejected in §1.3. Ground seat confirmed at Nit (missing one sentence of guidance). Documentation suggestion.
+- §1.2 path keyed on caller `covered`; §1.3 unlisted Completed+same-range+no-file case — Rejected 2/3: collision resolves to the designed `summary_conflict`, and the unlisted case falls through to a normal write; `covered` vs `rq.range` semantics are unchanged by H1. Ground seat confirmed at Nit. One-sentence clarification at most.
+- §3.1 ask rule matches only bare name and hook's absolute path — Rejected 3/3: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions says ask rules apply when any subcommand matches, including `cd /tmp && …` and subshells, so compound forms do prompt; alternate binary paths are the accepted "agent not working around the prefix match" class in §3.5.
+- §3.4 doctor ignores `defaultMode: bypassPermissions`/dontAsk and launch flags — Rejected 3/3: §3.5 names "prompt-skipping harness modes" as an accepted residual; doctor is specified as a presence check. Optional enhancement: report persistent `defaultMode` or suggest `disableBypassPermissionsMode`.
+- §4.1 in-band `## <label>` headers forgeable by part bodies — Rejected 3/3: every part is an approved instruction source shown verbatim in the diff; an author who can land text there can already write directives directly, so a forged header adds no capability; `data` is kept out of the instruction channel.
+- Goal vs §4.4 header (Beads, threads, proposals ungated) — Rejected 3/3: the Goal's second sentence scopes it to instruction sources (§2.1, InstructionSource); work and messaging channels are a different system the spec never claims to gate. One-line Goal clarification at most.
+- §2.4 warning; InstructionsKind mutate (no `reload_required`, no push to live sessions) — Rejected 3/3: delivery at start/clear/compaction is the stated contract; §2.4's warning names affected live seats at approval time; launch-time injection is listed out of scope.
+- §1.4/§2.4 no size bound; LCS O(n*m) in the daemon — Rejected 3/3: no availability requirement in the spec; single-user local daemon; instruction files are small by design (9000-byte hook cap). Implementation hardening note for the leaf at most.
+- §4.4; Goal "at session start, clear and compaction" (Codex SessionStart sources) — Rejected 3/3: official Codex hooks docs (https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/hooks) list sources `startup, resume, clear, compact, fork` and say compact-source hooks run after compaction before the next model request. Finding's third-party sources were stale.
+- Goal; Main challenges; §4.4 fallbacks (seats whose harness lacks the hook or triggers) — Rejected 3/3: the fallbacks are specified and bounded (§4.4 error → today's prompt, truncation → marker; acceptance criterion 6); the Codex-lacks-triggers premise is unsourced and contradicted by the docs above.
+- §4.1 `Result<RenderedInstructions, StoreError>`; §4.2 (seat --json on render error) — Rejected 3/3: §4.1 omits absent/empty parts and placeholders non-UTF-8, leaving only committed-store read failures, which already break /seat today; error mapping is a one-line implementation choice.
+- §1.4/§1.5 CLI/daemon version skew — Rejected 3/3: with `--summary-from` mutually exclusive with `--output`, an old daemon fails loudly with "needs {output}"; the silent-loss branch requires deviating from §1.1. Restart-after-upgrade is existing practice; optional CHANGELOG line.
